00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.090 align:start position:0%
after 18 months of negotiations and  last-minute concessions from member  states EU ministers of employment and  Social Affairs lived up to expectations  and agreed a compromise on how to  regulate workers posted from one EU  member state to another the new rules  which still need to be negotiated with  the European Parliament aim at better  protecting workers who are temporarily  posted abroad by their employers by  ensuring that minimum social rights are  respected it is a very important  agreement that will benefit all Member  States regardless of whether they are  hosting or sending member states posting  is key in such sectors as construction  agriculture transport and the IT  industry under the deal host Member  States would be free to decide the  verification measures to be imposed on  companies posting workers subject to  these requirements and controls being  proportionate and the European  Commission having been notified the  council also took stock of initiatives  fostering youth employment in the  different countries and exchange views  on the European semester 2014 in the  field of employment and social policy  finally the Lithuanian presidency of the  council inform ministers about the state  of play in negotiations on future EU  tobacco rules despite the split over  e-cigarettes both presidency and  Commission are still hoping for an  agreement before the end of the year
 

